the gate card says how long there is, and what silence will do

§1.6, **MISSING IN BOTH HEADS** — so this is not a port, it is the requirement
implemented against leticl's `550bc94`, which is the reference for it because
neither head had it and one of them had to go first. The operator was bitten the
same night: *"two gate cards timed out unanswered at 300 seconds with `not_run by
gate:timeout` — nothing on the card had said that was coming."*

Both facts were already on the wire — `DecisionRequested` carries `deadline` and
`on_timeout` (`event.rs:569-574`) — and this head drew neither. **And it already
drew a countdown on the secret card**, which is the shape of the whole omission: the
instrument existed in both trees and had never been pointed at the gate, where the
consequence of silence is a decision rather than a missing password.

## The three rulings

**1. A ladder, not a number.** The unit changes with the time left, so the reader gets
the precision the moment is worth: a countdown that ticks for five minutes is
furniture, and one that ticks for ten seconds is a pressure nobody asked for. Every
value below is asserted in `letibot-ui`'s test, taken from the reference's own table
rather than from my implementation, because two heads have to agree for the
requirement to be met:

    300s -> "expires in 5 min"      whole minutes, changing once a minute
    181s -> "expires in 4 min"      rounded UP
    120s -> "expires in 2 min"      the boundary is inclusive upward
    119s -> "1m59s left"            seconds from here
     59s -> "59s left"              whole seconds — `47s`, never `47.0s`

**Minutes round up and seconds do not**, and the direction is the point: a card must
never claim less time than there is. `3m00.001s` is closer to four minutes than to
three, and a card that says three has told the person they have less than they do.
**Getting that right needed a second pass**: my first cut floored to seconds and then
divided, which loses the millisecond and with it the minute it belonged to — 180,001 ms
read `3 min`. The rounding is on the milliseconds in one step
(`remaining_ms.div_ceil(60_000)`), and the boundary case is now a test.

**2. A card past its own deadline.** The case the operator actually hit. A card still
on the screen after its own deadline is one the daemon has already settled and this
head has not been told the outcome of, so it says so:

    past its deadline by 14s; the daemon has not said what became of it · if nobody answers, it RUNS anyway

**It never counts into negative seconds** — a number that means nothing and reads as a
rendering fault — and it **keeps the consequence clause**, because that clause is a
RULE and not an observation: an operator looking at an expired card with
`on_timeout: allow` learns the one thing they most need to from it.

**3. `deadline: null` draws nothing.** `null` is §11.5's *wait forever* — a policy,
not missing information — so there is no silence for a clause to describe.
**This is §13.2b answered in the other direction from `unreadable 0` on `/status`, and
deliberately**: that count IS shown at zero, because a head that does not count frames
it cannot read is a different head, while a countdown on an ask with no deadline is a
number nobody took. Both are asserted — including that the card is still otherwise
drawn, so "draws nothing" cannot be satisfied by drawing nothing at all.

## What silence does, in the daemon's own words

Below the options, because the order a person reads is the question, the choices, then
what happens if they do nothing:

    deny  -> if nobody answers, nothing runs
    allow -> if nobody answers, it RUNS anyway
    ask   -> if nobody answers, the guard model decides

**The upper case on `RUNS` is the point of the line.** It is the only one of the three
that does something nobody asked for, and an operator who walked away believing the
default was `deny` when it was `allow` has been told nothing at all by a clock.

**The "unknown word" arm has nothing to catch here, and saying which is which is the
part that matters.** The reference adds *"a word this head does not know draws no
clause rather than a guess"*; `OnTimeout` is an **enum on the wire** in this tree, so a
fourth value is not a word this function fails to recognise — it is a frame the
deserializer refuses, which lands in the `unreadable` bucket with everything else this
build cannot read (R3). A wrong consequence is worse than an absent one, and neither is
silence.

## The two-clocks trap: checked, and this head does **not** have it

leticl found a third instance of R13's trap while measuring the instrument it was sent
to copy: its secret card subtracted a **Unix** deadline from a **monotonic** clock and
had been drawing hundreds of thousands of seconds since it was written. The operator
asked whether mine does the same, and the answer is no — `SecretRequested.deadline` is
Unix millis and this head's clock is Unix millis (`bin::now_ms`, `driver::now_ms`,
both `SystemTime::UNIX_EPOCH`), so the subtraction is in one frame.

That was already asserted in `fe80602` rather than argued, and it is re-asserted here
against the ladder's own spellings. **It also turned up the one real defect on that
card**, which is worth recording: the countdown is the last field on that line and the
card *trims to the width*, so on a ~96-column terminal the number is cut off before it
can be read. Not fixed here — recorded, because a deadline a narrow screen cannot show
is the same family as everything else this queue is about.

## One instrument, which was the finding

`secret_lines` now calls the same `progress::countdown` the gate card does. `120s left`
was that card's old spelling and `expires in 2 min` is what the shared ladder makes of
it — the same number, and the two cards can no longer disagree about how long there is.

**And the repaint rate follows from the ladder rather than being chosen.** The finest
rung is one whole second, so a countdown needs one frame a second and not the ten a
spinner needs. The reference has a `live-frame-interval-ms` for this; **this head does
not need one and must not grow one** — `term.keys()` returns after ~100 ms of quiet
(`VMIN=0 VTIME=1`) and `Driver::tick` composes a frame unconditionally, which is the
same property that disproved the R13 unification in `fe80602`. Two heads, one
requirement, two mechanisms that are correct for different reasons — which is the
argument for requirements rather than shared implementations, and the second time
tonight it has earned its keep.

## Tests

- `a_countdown_changes_unit_with_the_time_left_and_never_overstates_the_pressure` and
  `the_minutes_rung_says_expires_and_the_seconds_rungs_say_left` in `letibot-ui` — the
  ladder's table, both rounding boundaries, no decimal on a whole second, no negative.
- `the_gate_card_says_how_long_there_is_and_what_silence_does` in `letibot-tui` — the
  ladder on the card, all three consequence clauses, the order (consequence after the
  choices), the expired sentence with its clause kept and `-` absent, and the null
  deadline drawing neither countdown nor clause while the card itself is still drawn.
- The secret card's test now asserts the shared spellings at four instants, including
  `0s left` for an expired one rather than a number counting backwards.

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **304 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (302 + 2).
`cargo test -p letibot-ui`: **101 passed, 0 failed**. All targets green on both.

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `letibot-harnessd`, `-sessionlog`,
`-tokencore` and `-turn` have not been run since `write.rs` changed in `65725ce` — a
run of all four together was started and then contended on the cargo lock with my own
concurrent builds, and it is still in flight as this is written. Nothing in this
commit touches them; the outstanding question is only whether any of their tests
depended on the refusal that `65725ce` removed, and `clauses.rs` — where that
dependency actually lived — is green. `rustfmt --check` still cannot run: it writes a
temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which only
the operator lifts.

## Not in this commit

**§1.7 — a question can be answered.** The reference landed it alongside §1.6 because
it was already in that tree. A's half is **checked, not assumed**, and it is what the
requirement says rather than the crash leticl had: `OpenDecision::choices` is read by
**nothing** in this head, and `match_option` iterates `options`, which is empty for a
question — so a question draws no choices and a typed line under one is held with
*"this ask offers no options"*. It does not TYPE-ERROR. It cannot be answered at all,
which is the same requirement unmet by a different failure, and it is the next item.
